---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const app = 'https://materials-ml-predictor.streamlit.app/';
---
<Base title="Machine learning for materials" description="A machine learning model that classifies alloys from their measured properties, built by Hridal out of interest, with a live app you can try.">
  <article class="case">
    <a class="case-back" href="/projects">All projects</a>
    <p class="case-kind">Personal project, October to December 2025</p>
    <h1>Machine learning for materials</h1>
    <p class="case-lede">A classification model that predicts what kind of alloy a sample is from its measured properties, and a live app that lets anyone try it.</p>

    <figure class="case-viewer">
      <div class="viewer-box app-box" id="appbox">
        <div class="app-poster">
          <p class="app-name">Materials ML predictor</p>
          <p class="app-sub">Enter a sample's properties and the model predicts its class.</p>
          <button class="btn solid" id="load-app">Open the app here</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>The app runs on Streamlit and can take a few seconds to wake up the first time. <a href={app} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open it in a new tab</a></figcaption>
    </figure>

    <dl class="case-facts">
      <div><dt>My role</dt><dd>Everything, from cleaning the data to building and deploying the app</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Tools</dt><dd>Python, scikit-learn, Streamlit</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Data</dt><dd>5,000 alloy samples, plus a second set of 400</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Result</dt><dd>100% accuracy from a single measurement</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <h2>The question</h2>
    <p>Classifying a material usually means running several lab measurements on every sample, which takes time and money. I wanted to know how many of those measurements actually matter, and whether a model could get to the same answer with far fewer of them.</p>

    <h2>What I did</h2>
    <p>I built a classification pipeline on 5,000 alloy samples and used feature importance alongside 5-fold cross-validation to see which measurements the model really relied on. On a second dataset of 400 samples I compared four different classifiers and settled on a random forest, which reached 100% accuracy on data it had never seen.</p>

    <h2>What I found</h2>
    <p>Band gap on its own matched the accuracy of the full model, which means the number of lab measurements needed per sample could drop from ten to one. A learning curve also showed that around 30 samples were enough to reach 70% accuracy, roughly 90% less data than had been collected in the first place.</p>

    <h2>Why it matters</h2>
    <p>For a lab, measuring one property instead of ten is a large saving in time and cost, and knowing that a small dataset is enough makes it much easier to start on a new material. Building the app on top meant the result became something people could use, not just a number in a notebook.</p>
  </article>
  <script>
    document.getElementById('load-app')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const box = document.getElementById('appbox')!;
      const f = document.createElement('iframe');
      f.src = 'https://materials-ml-predictor.streamlit.app/?embed=true'; f.title = 'Materials ML predictor app';
      box.replaceChildren(f);
    });
  </script>
</Base>
